at can end wildly  successfully but it can also cause a lot  of problems and you dilute yourself down  a lot and you know then you have a lot  of other people you're answering to so  that just was the journey that I took  you know I mean is I started it with  five grand I've never had any outside  funding and whatever money I made from  selling Spanx I just put back into the  business I love that start small think  big rule number six get off the  autopilot one of the things I also do is  I like to go up to people at Spanx and I  say I'm always trying to get people off  autopilot like if you think about it  everything we do almost everything we do  in life is autopilot we're doing things  exactly the way someone else  showed us how to do it or taught us how  to do it well if you're doing things  exactly the way everyone else is there's  no there's not going to be any real  significant change there's not going to  be a really meaningful potential  breakthrough so I always go up to people  at Sphinx and say if nobody showed you  how to do your job how would you be  doing it and I'll say just sit with that  you know I'm always like I'll stop  myself often and just say okay you know  this is how I've been doing it but is  this right and give yourself the time to  think about it so I ask people to do  that  rule number seven start with why the  best advice I would give a female  founder looking to launch a female  centric company or product is to start  with the why and continue to lead with  why and be very authentic in your  message stay vulnerable be yourself  through the process at Spanx when I  first started it I observed how  companies were treating me as a consumer  and I was very determined to be one of  my customers and to be authentically  connected to them  rule number eight hire your weaknesses  in the early days I mean as an  entrepreneur you're every department so  I was the before and after but model  literally I took a picture of my own but  took it to Kinkos got it laminated with  and without Spanx on stood in the stores  and I'm like hey look what this can do  for you I was I was like the packer and  shipper I was the head of sales I was  everything and you learned very quickly  what you're good at and what you're not  good at and what you enjoy and what you  don't enjoy and there's usually a  correlation between the two and so I  often say as soon as you can afford to  hire your weaknesses and a few years  into Spanx it was abundantly clear that  the lane that I needed to stay in was  inventing selling promoting Spanx  actually he's never advertised Spanx  it's 18 years old and the first time  we've ever even sampled an ad was to in  16 and so I was sort of the the  advertisement and and me being out in  the field talking about the product and  sharing the story of the why why we're  making this why it's better was a big  part of the formula and I needed someone  to be in  the company day-to-day running the daily  operations rule number nine solve a  problem you were a frustrated consumer I  was and you decided to try to solve a  problem what was the problem you were  trying to solve well I couldn't figure  out what to wear under white pants and I  was a frustrated consumer the  undergarment options at that time were  not great right we had underwear that  left a panty line shape girdles that  were really thick and uncomfortable and  then they invented the thong which just  put underwear exactly where we have been  trying to get it out of or so so I was  like yeah I don't know and listen at the  time that I invented Spanx and for the  guys in the audience or some of the  ladies who don't know what Spanx is it's  just I invented an undergarment that  filled the gap between traditional  underwear and those heavy-duty shapers  and I was interested in giving women a  smooth canvas like I love clothes I love  you know to be able to wear them but  just like a painter if you don't have  the right canvas it affects the whole  painting and the options weren't great  and they could be seen they left lines  they left bulges so that's what caused  me to to invent the first product which  was the first ever Spanx the footless  pantyhose and rule number 10 the last  wandering for a very special bonus clip  is focus on impact I would say for the  next hundred years I think entrepreneurs  and business people should be  considering two things one is the social  impact I'm really thrilled to see that  businesses are really having a social  conscious and what they're producing and  how they're producing it  but also a much more feminine approach  to business I think that business  traditionally has been very masculine in  the way that it's been done and I went  about Spanx and such a feminine way with  much more feminine principles and I  think when that's more balanced on the  planet we're all going to be better off  now I've got a special bonus script from  Sarah on how to find your true path that  I think you're gonna enjoy but before  that it's time for the three point  landing questions let's go from just  watching a video to taking action here  we go question number one where do you  just need to start small from your big  idea number two what would the most  courageous version of you do right now  and number three what's the weakness  that you need to hire for I had wanted  to be a lawyer my father was a trial  attorney and I used to actually ask to  get out of school growing up to watch  him and closing arguments and I debated  all through high school I debated in  college and I'm basically a really bad  test taker and so I bombed the LSAT and  I bombed it not once but twice and so it  set my life on on a different course for  you know I would say things like that  happen and then you end up you know like  those those moments in your life where  you think I can't believe this is  happening to me is oftentimes life's way  of nudging you and just letting you know  you're off course  if 